"], "rationale": "Utah's eleven-point Republican lean and the absence of any polling or expert ratings showing a competitive race keep the GOP nominee a clear favorite; headlines about a Republican succession contest and stronger approval for Trump-aligned heirs reinforce rather than erode that position.", "watch": "A fracture in the post-Trump Republican coalition or a significant independent or third-party bid could compress the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Utah sits well to the right of the nation overall, with a partisan lean of about eleven points toward Republicans, and the 2028 race there has drawn little competitive attention. No general-election polls or expert ratings are available for the state, so the outlook rests on fundamentals. The incumbent president is term-limited, making this an open race, which does little to alter Utah's structural Republican advantage.\n\nHeadlines point to a Republican succession contest, with Utah conservatives warming to potential heirs like J.D. Vance, and reporting suggesting Trump-aligned successors rate better among Utahns than Trump himself. National Democrats such as Cory Booker have begun courting Utah Democrats, but those are early primary-season signals rather than evidence of a competitive general election. Absent a fracture in the post-Trump Republican coalition or a significant independent bid, Utah is expected to remain in the Republican column.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1067, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1478, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1592, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1542, "margin": -11.0}]}